# unzip — real-world examples

## Basics

```sh
# Extract an archive into the current directory
unzip archive.zip

# Test integrity of an archive quietly
unzip -tq archive.zip

# List files and directories inside without extracting
unzip -l archive.zip
```

## Control where and what

```sh
# Extract into a specific directory
unzip archive.zip -d /srv/media/incoming/

# Extract a single file from the archive
unzip archive.zip path/inside/archive.conf

# Extract only PDFs, skipping everything else
unzip archive.zip '*.pdf'

# Overwrite existing files without prompting (-o), or never overwrite (-n)
unzip -o archive.zip
unzip -n archive.zip
```

## Homelab uses

```sh
# Peek inside a downloaded plugin bundle before trusting it
unzip -l ~/Downloads/grafana-plugin.zip

# Extract a subtitle pack straight into the movie folder on mediaserver
unzip -o subs.zip -d "/srv/media/movies/The Movie (2024)/"

# Unzip quietly in a script (no per-file chatter)
unzip -q backup-configs.zip -d /tmp/restore/

# Read a text file from an archive without extracting (-p pipes to stdout)
unzip -p archive.zip README.txt | less
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-l` | list contents |
| `-t` | test integrity |
| `-q` | quiet mode |
| `-d DIR` | extract into DIR |
| `-o` | overwrite without asking |
| `-n` | never overwrite existing files |
| `-p` | extract to stdout (pipe) |
| `-j` | junk paths — flatten directory structure |
| `-P PASS` | password (insecure — visible in process list) |
